      "text": "### Fleming, 1928 — The Original Observation"
    },
    {
      "id": "09-INDUSTRIAL-MYCOLOGY.md#007",
      "path": [
        "09-INDUSTRIAL-MYCOLOGY.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:mycology:09-industrial-mycology, git-history:mycology:09-industrial-mycology]",
        "canonical_path": "mycology/09-INDUSTRIAL-MYCOLOGY.md",
        "concepts": "[industrial, mycology]",
        "current_path": "mycology/09-INDUSTRIAL-MYCOLOGY.md",
        "id": "maxim:mycology:industrial-mycology",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "mycology",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[industrial, mycology]",
        "section": "mycology",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Industrial Mycology",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "```\nTHE ACCIDENT:\n  Alexander Fleming, St. Mary's Hospital, London, September 1928\n  Staphylococcus culture plate contaminated by mold\n  Observation: clear zone of inhibition around mold colony\n  Fleming's insight: mold produces diffusible bactericidal substance\n  Named it \"penicillin\" (from Penicillium notatum, the contaminant)"
    },
    {
      "id": "09-INDUSTRIAL-MYCOLOGY.md#008",
      "path": [
        "09-INDUSTRIAL-MYCOLOGY.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:mycology:09-industrial-mycology, git-history:mycology:09-industrial-mycology]",
        "canonical_path": "mycology/09-INDUSTRIAL-MYCOLOGY.md",
        "concepts": "[industrial, mycology]",
        "current_path": "mycology/09-INDUSTRIAL-MYCOLOGY.md",
        "id": "maxim:mycology:industrial-mycology",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "mycology",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[industrial, mycology]",
        "section": "mycology",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Industrial Mycology",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "WHAT FLEMING DID AND DID NOT DO:\n  Did: Identify the phenomenon; characterize antibacterial spectrum;\n       note it was non-toxic to animals; published 1929 (British Journal\n       of Experimental Pathology)\n  Did not: Purify or concentrate it; trial in patients; develop for\n           clinical use\n  → Fleming's strain produced tiny amounts; unstable; nobody could\n    work with it therapeutically for 10 years"
    },
    {
      "id": "09-INDUSTRIAL-MYCOLOGY.md#009",
      "path": [
        "09-INDUSTRIAL-MYCOLOGY.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:mycology:09-industrial-mycology, git-history:mycology:09-industrial-mycology]",
        "canonical_path": "mycology/09-INDUSTRIAL-MYCOLOGY.md",
        "concepts": "[industrial, mycology]",
        "current_path": "mycology/09-INDUSTRIAL-MYCOLOGY.md",
        "id": "maxim:mycology:industrial-mycology",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "mycology",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[industrial, mycology]",
        "section": "mycology",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Industrial Mycology",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "THE CHAIN/FLOREY TEAM (Oxford, 1938–1941):\n  Howard Florey (Australian pathologist) + Ernst Chain (German Jewish\n  biochemist refugee) + Norman Heatley (biochemist)\n  → Concentrated and purified penicillin (ammonium salt extraction)\n  → Animal trials 1940: mice challenged with streptococcal lethal dose;\n    treated animals survived; untreated died\n  → First human trial: Albert Alexander, police officer, February 1941\n    Severe septicemia from rose thorn scratch (face/scalp)\n    Penicillin: dramatic recovery → supply ran out → he died\n  → Demonstrated clinical efficacy; demonstrated the supply problem"
    },
    {
      "id": "09-INDUSTRIAL-MYCOLOGY.md#010",
      "path": [
        "09-INDUSTRIAL-MYCOLOGY.md"
